cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use this experiment or its data, please cite it as below."
title: "The Attention Ablation Test"
abstract: >-
  A controlled test of whether self-attention decides if a false statement in the input
  becomes a confident error. Five size-matched Pythia (attention) and Mamba (state space)
  pairs trained on the same data with the same tokenizer, plus four further attention
  models (14 in total), are compared on free recall (200 SimpleQA questions) and on a
  999-item context-conflict probe. Free recall does not change; the attention models
  repeat a planted falsehood more often from 410M upward (2.8B: 650 vs 566 of 666,
  p = 2.5e-20), including on facts both models knew.
